---
/**
 * SiteNav.astro — the desktop section navigation for the landing page.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A SECOND `<nav>` AND NOT THE EXISTING ONE
 * -----------------------------------------------------
 * The roadmap's first choice was to extend the existing
 * `<nav aria-label="Primary navigation">`, and that is geometrically impossible.
 * That element is the DRAWER: `u-viewport-fixed--right`, i.e. `position: fixed;
 * right: 0; height: 100dvh`. A section bar needs `position: sticky; top: 0;
 * width: 100%`. One element cannot be both, and the drawer's geometry is load
 * bearing — `e2e/test-drawer.mjs` proves a CLOSED drawer is off-screen by
 * measuring that element's `rect.x` (expected: the viewport width). Turning the
 * nav into a wrapper around both would make `rect.x` 0 and quietly delete that
 * proof.
 *
 * So this is a second landmark, and the roadmap's objection to that — "two
 * navigation landmarks with no relationship, the screen reader announces two
 * menus and the user cannot tell them apart" — is answered by giving it a
 * DIFFERENT, meaningful label. ARIA does not cap the number of `nav` landmarks;
 * it requires them to be distinguishable. "Primary navigation" (the site's
 * actions: install, login, admin) and "Navigasi halaman" (this page's sections)
 * are two different navigations, not two names for one. (Language used to be
 * listed among the primary actions too; it moved to the drawer exclusively on
 * 2026-09-25 and is no longer reachable from this bar.)
 *
 * AND THE HOLE THAT OPENED IS CLOSED IN THE SAME CHANGE. The real danger was
 * never "two navs" — it was that `e2e/test-drawer.mjs` selects the drawer with
 * `querySelector` on a single label and would therefore measure the WRONG element
 * if a second one shared that label, passing while proving nothing. That gate now
 * asserts the count of that selector is exactly 1, so the same-label case cannot
 * slip through again.
 *
 * WHY DESKTOP ONLY. The drawer already covers mobile, and `hidden lg:block` keeps
 * this out of the 390px layout entirely — which also keeps it out of the drawer
 * gate's viewport.
 *
 * WHY NO GLASS. DESIGN.md §3.6 restricts `backdrop-filter` to surfaces over the
 * banner artwork. This bar sits over ordinary page content, so it is a solid
 * `bg-canvas` with a bottom border: legible in both themes, one paint, and it
 * cannot fall out of step with the theme the way a translucent layer can.
 *
 * WHY THE FOUR LINKS ARE WRITTEN OUT AND NOT LOOPED
 * -------------------------------------------------
 * They are a FIXED set, not data — and looping in an `.astro` template is the one
 * pattern that breaks this repo's zero-unresolved invariant
 * (`indexer/src/build.test.ts:428`), because the indexer resolves template
 * interpolations against the frontmatter module scope only, so a `.map()`
 * callback parameter is reported as an unknown global. Four literal items cost
 * nothing and keep the invariant; a loop would have cost a red gate. (This file's
 * first draft did exactly that and was rewritten.)
 *
 * WHY THE ITEM LIST IS SHORT. It lists only sections that EXIST — a nav item
 * pointing at a fragment that does not resolve is a link that looks live and does
 * nothing, and this list once shipped exactly that (the Lowongan item, below).
 *
 * AN ITEM IS ADDED WHEN ITS SECTION LANDS, and both halves of that are load
 * bearing: the gate's expected set and the narrative comments below must move
 * together with the markup, or the comments start describing a page that no
 * longer exists. That is what happened to the two notes under the list — they
 * still said `layanan` was an absent hidden tab panel, ten lines above the
 * `<a href="#layanan" data-nav-link="layanan">` that had been there all along.
 * The markup was right; the prose had gone stale and outlived its subject.
 *
 * The `data-nav-link` values must match the section ids in index.astro.
 */
const LINK_CLASS =
  'inline-flex items-center px-3 py-2 rounded-control text-body-sm font-bold text-fg-muted hover:text-fg hover:bg-surface aria-[current=true]:text-accent-sky aria-[current=true]:bg-surface transition-colors';
---

<nav
  data-site-nav
  data-lang-aria="profile.nav_aria"
  aria-label="Navigasi halaman"
  class="hidden lg:block sticky top-0 z-sticky w-full bg-canvas border-b border-line"
>
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 flex items-center gap-1 h-14">
    <ul class="flex items-center gap-1 min-w-0">
      <li>
        <!-- POINTS AT THE PAGE, not a fragment. It was a fragment anchor to the
             lowongan table, but that table moved to its own `/loker` route (see
             the note in index.astro) so NO element on `/` carries that id. The
             link therefore looked live and did nothing — the exact defect the
             comment below says this nav refuses to ship, sitting two lines above
             it. Found by `e2e:site-nav` once that gate could connect at all; it
             had been unable to reach an IPv6-only preview (see its BASE note).

             This is why `data-nav-link` is dropped: that attribute drives the
             scroll-spy current-marker, and there is no section to mark. Keeping
             it would make the observer look for a target that never matches.

             THIS ITEM IS NOW THE PAGE'S ONLY LINK TO `/loker`. The duplicate CTA
             button that used to sit to the right was removed by the owner ruling
             of 2026-09-24 — see the note where it stood.

             NOTE ON THIS COMMENT'S WORDING: it deliberately does NOT spell out
             the current-marker attribute name, NOR the dead fragment it replaced.
             `e2e:site-nav` counts both literals in the SERVED document, and an
             earlier version of this comment contained them — so a COMMENT made
             the gate report a hand-written marker that did not exist (measured:
             0 attribute-form occurrences, 1 prose match). The gate is right to
             forbid these tokens; it just cannot tell prose from markup, so the
             prose avoids them. The dead fragment was re-introduced by the same
             mistake a second time, which is why it is now named in words. -->
        <a href="/loker" data-lang="profile.nav_loker" class={LINK_CLASS}>Lowongan</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#program" data-nav-link="program" data-lang="profile.nav_program" class={LINK_CLASS}>Program</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#alur" data-nav-link="alur" data-lang="profile.nav_alur" class={LINK_CLASS}>Alur</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#fasilitas" data-nav-link="fasilitas" data-lang="profile.nav_fasilitas" class={LINK_CLASS}>Fasilitas</a>
      </li>
      <!-- Added when S7 shipped. Until then this item was deliberately absent:
           a nav link pointing at a fragment that does not resolve looks live and
           does nothing. `#layanan` was absent for the same reason while it was
           still a hidden tab panel; it is a real section now, so the item below
           is present and resolves (`index.astro:156`). -->
      <li>
        <a href="#tentang" data-nav-link="tentang" data-lang="profile.nav_tentang" class={LINK_CLASS}>Tentang</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#layanan" data-nav-link="layanan" data-lang="profile.nav_layanan" class={LINK_CLASS}>Layanan</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-2 shrink-0">
      {/* THE LANGUAGE TOGGLE IS GONE FROM THE NAV — owner ruling 2026-09-25.
          The requirement is "the JP toggle only appears inside the hamburger
          drawer", and one entry point per control beats the convenience of a
          second switch up here for business partners reading /. The DRAWER's
          toggle (App.tsx) is now the only one on every route, including this
          one. The `data-nav-lang` / `data-nav-lang-label` wiring in this file's
          <script> was removed with it — a listener bound to a selector that no
          longer matches is the dead-control pattern this repo refuses to ship,
          in mirror image. The `ml-auto` wrapper keeps its other child (the CTA
          below), and a single-child flex row lays out identically. */}
      {/* THE DUPLICATE /loker CTA BUTTON IS GONE, NOT RELOCATED. The nav used to
          carry TWO paths to the vacancy list — the "Lowongan" list item above and
          this pink pill. Owner ruling 2026-09-24: `/` is a company profile for
          MoU/business partners, so it keeps EXACTLY ONE link to /loker and the
          list item above is it. The pill was the candidate-facing duplicate; a
          second path is what the region rule exists to notice. */}
      <button
        type="button"
        data-nav-login
        data-lang="header.login"
        class="inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 rounded-pill bg-sky-700 hover:bg-sky-800 text-white text-body-sm font-bold transition-colors"
      >
        Login Pelamar
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>

<script>
  /* ── Login ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The modal lives in the App island's state, which a static component cannot
     reach. `asj-kandidat-login` is the event the header's own Login button
     already dispatches, so both entry points run the same code path.

     NOTE: the language wiring (`langStore` / `toggleLang` and the
     `[data-nav-lang]` click listener) lived here until 2026-09-25 and was
     removed with the nav's language button — see the comment at the button's
     old site. The nav no longer touches the language store at all. */
  document.querySelector('[data-nav-login]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    window.dispatchEvent(new Event('asj-kandidat-login'));
  });

  /* ── Active section ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Driven by scroll POSITION, not `:hover` — the point is to answer "where am I
     on this page", and a hover highlight answers a different question.

     IntersectionObserver over a band rather than a scroll listener: the band is
     the strip just below the bar (`rootMargin` -56px for the bar, -70% at the
     bottom so only the top third of the viewport counts). No per-frame work and
     no `getBoundingClientRect` on every scroll event.

     `for...of` rather than `.map()` on purpose — same reason as the link list
     above. Script blocks are parsed separately today, but not depending on that
     is cheaper than discovering it changed.

     ⚠ AND NO TYPESCRIPT GENERICS IN THIS SCRIPT. The indexer scans an `.astro`
     file — INCLUDING its script blocks and its comments — for capitalised tags,
     so a generic type argument reads as a component tag. Two of them here (the
     generic form of `querySelectorAll`, and a `Set` annotated with a DOM
     interface) produced TWO unresolved `template-component` records and turned
     `build.test.ts:516` red. Measured, not guessed. A cast and type inference do
     the same job with no angle brackets — and that is also why this paragraph
     describes the offending forms instead of quoting them: quoting them here
     re-creates the defect, which is exactly what the first version of this
     comment did. */
  const links: HTMLElement[] = [];
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('[data-nav-link]')) links.push(el as HTMLElement);

  const sections: Element[] = [];
  for (const link of links) {
    const target = document.getElementById(link.getAttribute('data-nav-link') ?? '');
    if (target) sections.push(target);
  }

  if (sections.length && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    const visible = new Set();
    const sync = () => {
      for (const link of links) {
        const target = document.getElementById(link.getAttribute('data-nav-link') ?? '');
        const isCurrent = target ? visible.has(target) : false;
        if (isCurrent) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true');
        else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
      }
    };
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const entry of entries) {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) visible.add(entry.target);
          else visible.delete(entry.target);
        }
        sync();
      },
      { rootMargin: '-56px 0px -70% 0px', threshold: 0 },
    );
    for (const section of sections) observer.observe(section);
  }
</script>
